wounded.” “It is an interesting question,” said Lord Henry, who found an exquisite

205
00:18:03,680 --> 00:18:09,478
pleasure in playing on the lad’s unconscious egotism, “an extremely interesting

206
00:18:09,478 --> 00:18:15,959
question. I fancy that the true explanation is this: It often happens that the real

207
00:18:15,959 --> 00:18:21,040
tragedies of life occur in such an inartistic manner that they hurt us by

208
00:18:21,040 --> 00:18:27,719
their crude violence, their absolute incoherence, their absurd want of meaning,

209
00:18:27,719 --> 00:18:32,840
their entire lack of style. They affect us just as vulgarity

210
00:18:32,840 --> 00:18:39,040
affects us. They give us an impression of sheer brute force, and we revolt against

211
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:44,559
that. Sometimes, however, a tragedy that possesses artistic elements of beauty

212
00:18:44,559 --> 00:18:50,318
crosses our lives. If these elements of beauty are real, the whole thing simply

213
00:18:50,318 --> 00:18:53,559
appeals to our sense of dramatic effect.

214
00:18:53,559 --> 00:18:58,120
Suddenly we find that we are no longer the actors, but the spectators of the

215
00:18:58,120 --> 00:19:04,680
play. Or rather we are both. We watch ourselves, and the mere one th of the

216
00:19:04,680 --> 00:19:11,279
spectacle enthralls us. In the present case, what is it that has really

217
00:19:11,279 --> 00:19:18,600
happened someone has killed herself for love of you. I wish that I had ever had such an

218
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:22,879
experience. It would have made me in love with love for the rest of my life. The

219
00:19:22,879 --> 00:19:26,920
people who have adored me— —there have not been very many, but

220
00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:31,079
there have been some— —have always insisted on living on, long after I had

221
00:19:31,079 --> 00:19:35,600
ceased to care for them, or they to care for me. They have become stout and

222
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:42,318
tedious, and when I meet them, they go in at once for reminiscences. That awful memory of woman!

223
00:19:42,318 --> 00:19:47,398
What a fearful thing it is! And what an utter intellectual stagnation it reveals!

224
00:19:47,398 --> 00:19:51,760
One should absorb the color of life, but one should never remember its details.

225
00:19:51,760 --> 00:19:57,520
Details are always vulgar.” “I must sew poppies in my garden,” sighed

226
00:19:57,520 --> 00:20:03,879
Dorian. “There is no necessity,” rejoined his companion. “Life has always poppies in

227
00:20:03,879 --> 00:20:09,079
her her hands. Of course, now and then things linger. I once wore nothing but

228
00:20:09,079 --> 00:20:15,040
violets all through one season, as a form of artistic mourning for a romance that would not

229
00:20:15,040 --> 00:20:22,279
die. Ultimately, however, it did die. I forget what killed it. I think it

230
00:20:22,279 --> 00:20:26,920
was her proposing to sacrifice the whole world for me. That is always a dreadful

231
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:34,318
moment. It fills one with the terror of eternity. Well— —would you believe it?—

232
00:20:34,318 --> 00:20:39,520
?—a week ago, at Lady Hampshire’s, I found myself seated at dinner next to the lady

233
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:44,639
in question, and she insisted on going over the whole thing again, and digging

234
00:20:44,639 --> 00:20:51,318
up the past, and raking up the future. I had buried my romance in a bed of

